1. PostgreSQL Architecture & Query Processing
1.1 Connection-to-result flow
Application
   │
   │ SQL connection / query
   ▼
PostgreSQL server
   │
   ▼
Connection manager / postmaster
   │
   ├── Check pg_hba.conf
   │      └── Authentication / access allowed?
   │
   ▼
Backend process
   │
   ▼
Parser
   ├── Lexing / syntax check
   └── Parse tree
   ▼
Analyzer / Transformer
   ├── Resolve tables / columns / data types
   └── Query tree
   ▼
Rewriter
   └── Rules / view expansion
   ▼
Planner / Optimizer
   ├── Seq Scan
   ├── Index Scan
   ├── Bitmap Scan
   ├── Nested Loop
   ├── Hash Join
   └── Merge Join
   │
   └── Choose lowest estimated cost
   ▼
Executor
   ├── Shared buffers / storage
   └── Return rows
   ▼
Application
⭐ Connection → pg_hba.conf → Backend → Parse → Analyze → Rewrite → Plan → Execute → Result
1.2 Important architecture components
Component	Purpose
Postmaster / main server process	Manages connections and background processes.
Backend process	Handles a client session/query.
Shared buffers	PostgreSQL's main shared cache for data pages.
WAL buffers	Memory area where WAL records are held before being written to WAL files.
Heap	Main table storage.
Index	Separate access structure used to find table rows efficiently.
TOAST	Stores oversized column values outside the normal row storage.
Background processes	Handle tasks such as WAL writing, checkpoints, autovacuum and statistics/maintenance work.
1.3 pg_hba.conf vs listen_addresses
Parameter/file	Question answered
listen_addresses	On which server IP interfaces should PostgreSQL listen?
pg_hba.conf	Which client/database/user/address is allowed and how is it authenticated?
listen_addresses accepts IP addresses or '*' — not CIDR subnet ranges. CIDR ranges belong in the ADDRESS field of pg_hba.conf.
2. PostgreSQL Security, Users, Roles & Privileges
2.1 Three security layers
Layer	Question	Main control
1. Connection	Can you connect at all?	pg_hba.conf + listen_addresses
2. Database/schema	What can you use after connecting?	CONNECT, CREATE, USAGE
3. Object	What can you do to the data?	SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, etc.
2.2 Common pg_hba.conf methods
Method	Meaning
trust	Allows connection without password; dangerous for production.
password	Password authentication; use securely, preferably with SSL.
md5	Legacy password authentication.
scram-sha-256	Modern password authentication; preferred for new setups.
SELECT pg_reload_conf();

REVOKE CONNECT, CREATE ON DATABASE pdb FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE pdb TO app_user;

GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO app_user;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON employee TO app_user;
2.3 Users, roles and groups
Since PostgreSQL 8.1, users, roles and groups are represented by the same underlying role system. The practical difference is mainly default attributes.
Command	Default behavior
CREATE USER	Creates a role with LOGIN by default.
CREATE ROLE	Creates a role with NOLOGIN by default.
CREATE GROUP	Same underlying role concept; NOLOGIN by default.
2.4 Important role attributes
Attribute	Meaning
LOGIN	Role can connect to PostgreSQL.
SUPERUSER	Bypasses normal permission checks.
CREATEDB	Can create databases.
CREATEROLE	Can create/manage roles.
REPLICATION	Can perform replication-related connections/operations.
PASSWORD	Authentication credential for password-based login.
2.5 Predefined roles to remember
Role	Interview meaning
pg_database_owner	Dynamic role containing the current database owner.
pg_read_all_stats	Can view statistics/activity for other users.
pg_read_all_settings	Can view configuration settings, including normally restricted settings.
pg_read_all_data	Read-only access to all tables/views/sequences and schema usage; still needs CONNECT.
pg_write_all_data	Write access counterpart to pg_read_all_data.
pg_monitor	Bundle of monitoring-related predefined roles.
pg_signal_backend	Allows permitted cancellation/termination of other non-superuser backends.
pg_checkpoint	Allows a non-superuser to run CHECKPOINT.
pg_maintain	Maintenance operations such as VACUUM, ANALYZE, CLUSTER, REINDEX and related operations without table ownership; added in PG17.
pg_read_server_files	Read server-side files.
pg_write_server_files	Write/export to server-side files.
pg_execute_server_program	Run server-side programs through supported operations such as COPY PROGRAM.
pg_use_reserved_connections	Use reserved connection slots when normal connection capacity is exhausted.
2.6 Useful security/monitoring views
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_activity;
SELECT * FROM pg_settings;
SELECT * FROM pg_db_role_settings;
SELECT * FROM pg_shadow;   -- superuser-restricted view; pg_authid is underlying source
⭐ pg_hba.conf = can I connect?  Privileges = what can I do after connecting?
3. PostgreSQL Configuration
3.1 Main configuration files
File	Purpose
postgresql.conf	Main instance-level configuration.
postgresql.auto.conf	Stores ALTER SYSTEM changes; read after postgresql.conf and therefore overrides it.
pg_hba.conf	Client authentication rules; not managed by ALTER SYSTEM.
pg_ident.conf	Maps external/OS usernames to PostgreSQL roles when used with pg_hba.conf map rules.
3.2 Split configuration
include_dir = 'conf.d'
include_file = 'memory.conf'
include = 'autovacuum.conf'
•	include_dir can contain multiple configuration files; files are read in filename order.
•	include_file refers to one file. For multiple specific files, use multiple include_file/include directives or a directory.
•	ALTER SYSTEM writes to postgresql.auto.conf.
ALTER SYSTEM RESET work_mem;
ALTER SYSTEM RESET ALL;

SELECT pg_reload_conf();

SELECT name, setting, sourcefile, sourceline
FROM pg_settings;
3.3 pg_settings vs pg_db_role_settings
View	Use
pg_settings	Shows the effective runtime setting and useful metadata such as source file and context.
pg_db_role_settings	Shows settings applied specifically through ALTER DATABASE ... SET and ALTER ROLE ... SET.
3.4 Configuration context — interview version
Context	Meaning	Examples
internal	Fixed; cannot be changed at runtime.	block_size, server_version
postmaster	Requires full restart.	shared_buffers, max_connections, wal_level, port
sighup	Change config + reload.	many logging/checkpoint settings
backend / superuser-backend	Takes effect at backend/session start, with privilege requirements depending on parameter.	session-start parameters
superuser	Superuser can change it in a session.	parameter-dependent
user	User can SET it for their session.	work_mem, search_path, statement_timeout
⭐ Before changing a parameter: SELECT name, setting, context FROM pg_settings WHERE name='parameter_name';
4. Memory & Important Performance Parameters
Parameter	Purpose	Remember
shared_buffers	PostgreSQL's internal cache.	Actual memory allocation.
work_mem	Memory per operation such as sort/hash; can be used multiple times by one query and across sessions.	Dynamic; do not multiply it only by max_connections without considering actual operations/concurrency.
maintenance_work_mem	Memory for maintenance operations such as VACUUM and index creation.	Maintenance, not normal query sorting.
autovacuum_work_mem	Memory used by autovacuum workers for maintenance.	Per autovacuum worker.
effective_cache_size	Planner estimate of cache available from PostgreSQL + OS cache.	Does NOT allocate memory.
temp_buffers	Memory for temporary tables per session.	Session-specific.
huge_pages	Uses larger memory pages when supported by OS.	Reduces page-management overhead.
max_worker_processes	Cluster-wide ceiling for background/parallel workers.	Upper ceiling.
max_parallel_workers	Parallel worker ceiling.	Must fit under max_worker_processes.
max_parallel_workers_per_gather	Max parallel workers for one Gather/Gather Merge.	One query's parallelism ceiling.
⭐ effective_cache_size is a planner estimate, not allocated RAM. Higher values can make index plans look cheaper; lower values can make sequential scans more attractive.
4.1 Parallel worker hierarchy
max_worker_processes ≥ max_parallel_workers ≥ max_parallel_workers_per_gather
•	Each parallel worker is a separate process with its own CPU and memory usage.
•	The optimizer decides whether parallelism is worthwhile.
•	Test session-level changes before making cluster-wide changes.
5. Query Performance & EXPLAIN
5.1 EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE
Command	What it does
EXPLAIN	Planner estimates the execution plan; query is not executed.
EXPLAIN ANALYZE	Executes the query and reports actual rows/time/loops in addition to estimates.
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)	Adds buffer activity such as shared hit/read/dirtied/written.
Important: EXPLAIN ANALYZE on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE really executes the statement. For a safe test, use a transaction and ROLLBACK.
BEGIN;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) UPDATE employee SET salary = 50000 WHERE employee_id = 10;
ROLLBACK;
5.2 Buffer terminology
Metric	Meaning
shared hit	Page already found in shared_buffers.
shared read	Page had to be read from storage/OS cache into shared_buffers.
shared dirtied	Page was modified in shared_buffers.
shared written	Dirty page was written out during the query.
5.3 Estimated vs actual rows
ANALYZE updates pg_statistic information used by the planner. If estimated rows and actual rows differ greatly, stale or unrepresentative statistics may cause a poor plan.
ANALYZE employee;
VACUUM (ANALYZE) employee;
⭐ Estimated rows = what planner expected. Actual rows = what execution really produced. A large mismatch is a strong troubleshooting clue.
5.4 Planner cost parameters
Parameter	Meaning
random_page_cost	Estimated cost of random page access relative to sequential access.
seq_page_cost	Estimated cost of sequential page access.
cpu_tuple_cost	CPU cost for processing a row.
cpu_index_tuple_cost	CPU cost for processing an index entry.
effective_cache_size	Planner estimate of cache availability.
5.5 Work_mem and disk spill
Sorts and hash operations can use work_mem. If an operation needs more memory than available for that operation, it can spill to temporary files.
⭐ High temp-file usage or disk-based sorts/hashes can be a sign that work_mem is insufficient for that workload — but increasing it globally can also increase memory pressure.
6. Scan Types & Join Methods
6.1 Sequential Scan vs Index Scan vs Bitmap Scan
Method	Simple meaning	Good fit
Seq Scan	Reads the table sequentially.	Large portion of table must be read; small tables; planner cost favors it.
Index Scan	Uses an index to find matching entries, then fetches heap rows.	Selective lookups.
Bitmap Index Scan + Bitmap Heap Scan	Builds a bitmap of matching heap locations, then fetches table pages efficiently.	Many matching rows where individual random index lookups would be expensive.
⭐ B-tree = index structure. Bitmap Index Scan = execution method that can use an index to find many matching rows.
6.2 Join methods
Join	How it works	Good fit / tuning clue
Nested Loop	For each outer row, find matching inner rows.	Small outer input + indexed inner join column.
Hash Join	Build hash table from one input, probe it with the other.	Equality joins; enough work_mem reduces batching.
Merge Join	Processes two sorted inputs together.	Large inputs where sorted/indexed inputs are useful.
6.3 Hash join memory
The hash table uses work_mem multiplied by hash_mem_multiplier. If the hash table does not fit, PostgreSQL may use multiple batches, which can increase execution time.
SET enable_hashjoin = off;
SET enable_mergejoin = off;
SET enable_nestloop = off;
These settings are useful for testing/comparison, not as a normal tuning solution.
7. PostgreSQL Indexes
Index	Best fit	Memory line
B-tree	Equality, range, ordering, BETWEEN, IN, IS NULL.	Default general-purpose index.
Hash	Equality only.	Narrow use case; no ordering/range.
GiST	Ranges, overlap, containment, geometric/nearest-neighbor workloads.	Flexible generalized search tree.
SP-GiST	Naturally partitionable/non-balanced data such as prefixes/IP structures.	Space-partitioned access method.
GIN	Arrays, JSONB containment, full-text style multi-value searches.	Value → matching rows.
BRIN	Huge naturally ordered tables such as time-series data.	Tiny summary index; relies on physical correlation.
7.1 Useful index commands
CREATE INDEX idx_emp_salary ON employees(salary);
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON table_name USING btree (column_name);
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_name ON table_name (column_name);

SELECT indexname, indexdef
FROM pg_indexes
WHERE tablename = 'table_name';

SELECT *
FROM pg_stat_user_indexes
WHERE relname = 'table_name';

SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('idx_name'));

REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_name;
7.2 Index strategy
•	Start with the query pattern, not simply the data type.
•	WHERE / JOIN / ORDER BY columns are common index candidates.
•	Avoid unnecessary indexes because indexes add storage and write/maintenance cost.
•	An index existing does not guarantee the planner will use it.
•	Use EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) to verify actual behavior.
8. VACUUM, ANALYZE, AUTOVACUUM & Bloat
Operation	Purpose	Lock / space behavior
VACUUM	Removes dead tuples and makes space reusable inside the table; updates FSM/VM.	Allows normal reads/writes; does not normally return file space to OS.
VACUUM FULL	Rewrites the table and returns unused space to OS.	ACCESS EXCLUSIVE; blocks normal activity on that table.
ANALYZE	Refreshes planner statistics.	Statistics maintenance.
pg_repack	Online table/index reorganization with a short final lock.	Useful when long blocking from VACUUM FULL is unacceptable.
8.1 Autovacuum trigger formula
VACUUM trigger =
autovacuum_vacuum_threshold
+
(autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor × estimated table rows)
Example: threshold 50 + (0.2 × 10,000 rows) = about 2,050 dead tuples.
Parameter	Purpose
autovacuum	Enable automatic vacuum/analyze.
autovacuum_max_workers	Number of autovacuum workers.
autovacuum_naptime	Delay between autovacuum launcher checks.
autovacuum_vacuum_threshold	Fixed dead-tuple threshold.
autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor	Fraction of table size added to threshold.
autovacuum_analyze_threshold	Fixed threshold for analyze.
autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor	Fraction used for analyze trigger.
autovacuum_freeze_max_age	Age threshold related to transaction ID freezing.
8.2 Why autovacuum may not clean dead tuples
•	Long-running transactions can keep old row versions visible.
•	Hot standby feedback can prevent cleanup needed by standby queries.
•	Replication slots can retain WAL and contribute to operational pressure.
•	Autovacuum settings may be too conservative.
•	Autovacuum may be disabled or unable to keep up.
•	Investigate pg_stat_activity, pg_stat_user_tables, pg_stat_progress_vacuum, pg_replication_slots and relevant settings.
9. Transactions, MVCC, Locks & XID Wraparound
9.1 MVCC
PostgreSQL uses Multi-Version Concurrency Control. UPDATE creates a new row version rather than simply overwriting the old version. Readers use transaction snapshots to determine which version they can see.
System column	Meaning
xmin	Transaction ID that created the row version.
xmax	Transaction ID associated with deleting/updating the row version.
ctid	Physical tuple location; can change when a row is updated.
⭐ MVCC allows readers and writers to operate concurrently by keeping multiple row versions.
9.2 XID wraparound
Transaction IDs are 32-bit and wrap around. PostgreSQL freezes old tuples so very old transaction IDs are not confused with new transaction IDs after wraparound.
SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid)
FROM pg_database;

SELECT relname, age(relfrozenxid)
FROM pg_class;
⭐ VACUUM/AUTOVACUUM + freezing protects PostgreSQL from transaction ID wraparound.
9.3 Locks and deadlocks
Concept	Meaning
Row-level lock	Protects affected rows from conflicting operations.
Table-level lock	Controls access to table-level operations.
Blocked session	Waiting for another session's lock/resource.
Blocking session	Holding the resource needed by another session.
Deadlock	Two or more transactions wait for each other in a cycle.
SELECT pid, xact_start, wait_event, wait_event_type,
       pg_blocking_pids(pid)
FROM pg_stat_activity;
Before terminating a session, identify what it is doing. Prefer pg_cancel_backend() for a softer query cancellation; use pg_terminate_backend() when the whole backend must be terminated and it is operationally safe.
10. PostgreSQL Storage Internals
10.1 Data directory — important folders
Directory	Purpose
base/	Per-database relation files.
global/	Cluster-wide system catalogs.
pg_tblspc/	Links to user-defined tablespace locations.
pg_xact/	Transaction commit/abort status.
pg_commit_ts/	Commit timestamps when enabled.
pg_twophase/	Prepared two-phase transaction state.
pg_subtrans/	Subtransaction → parent transaction relationships.
pg_replslot/	Physical and logical replication slot state.
pg_logical/	Logical decoding working data.
pg_dynshmem/	Dynamic shared-memory related files.
pg_stat/ / pg_stat_tmp/	Statistics-related storage; newer versions use shared memory heavily for stats.
10.2 Relation files
Tables and indexes have relation files identified by relfilenode. Large relations are split into segment files.
SELECT pg_relation_filepath('table_name');
File	Purpose
main relation file	Main table/index data.
_fsm	Free Space Map — tracks free space available on pages.
_vm	Visibility Map — tracks pages marked all-visible and all-frozen where applicable.
10.3 Visibility Map — simple explanation
Heap page 1 → all-visible ✓
Heap page 2 → not all-visible ✗
Heap page 3 → all-visible ✓
All-visible means PostgreSQL knows that tuples on that heap page are visible to all relevant transactions, so an Index-Only Scan can often avoid visiting the heap just to perform visibility checks.
⭐ VM = visibility. FSM = free space.
10.4 Tablespaces and schemas
Concept	Remember
pg_default	Default tablespace for normal objects.
pg_global	Shared cluster-wide catalogs.
temp_tablespaces	Where temporary objects/sort spill files can be placed.
Schema	Logical namespace inside a database.
pg_catalog	System catalogs/functions.
information_schema	Standard SQL metadata views.
public	General-purpose schema; PostgreSQL 15+ changes default ownership/CREATE behavior.
11. WAL, Checkpoints & Background Writing
11.1 WAL
Write-Ahead Logging means required WAL records are written before the corresponding data pages are considered durable. WAL is central to crash recovery, replication and PITR.
Item	Purpose
pg_wal/	Stores WAL segment files.
pg_waldump	Reads/decodes WAL records for investigation.
pg_stat_wal	WAL generation/flush statistics.
pg_switch_wal()	Forces a WAL segment switch.
min_wal_size	Minimum WAL disk space PostgreSQL tries to keep available for reuse.
max_wal_size	Soft WAL size target that influences when checkpoints are triggered.
11.2 Checkpoints
Checkpoint trigger	Meaning
Time-based	checkpoint_timeout interval reached.
Size-based	WAL volume reaches max_wal_size.
Manual	CHECKPOINT command.
checkpoint_completion_target spreads checkpoint writes across the checkpoint interval to smooth I/O.
⭐ Prefer a workload where checkpoints are mostly timed rather than constantly requested because max_wal_size is being reached too quickly.
11.3 Background writer / checkpointer / WAL writer
Component	High-level responsibility
WAL writer	Writes WAL records from WAL buffers toward WAL files.
Checkpointer	Writes dirty data pages as part of checkpoints.
Background writer	Writes some dirty buffers in the background to keep clean buffers available.
11.4 pg_stat_bgwriter clues
Metric	Interpretation
checkpoints_timed	Timed checkpoints.
checkpoints_req	Requested/forced checkpoints; high values can indicate WAL pressure.
buffers_checkpoint	Buffers written during checkpoints.
buffers_clean	Buffers written by background writer.
buffers_backend	Buffers written directly by client backends; high values can indicate pressure.
12. Physical Streaming Replication
12.1 Architecture
PRIMARY
  │
  │ WAL
  ▼
WAL Sender
  │
  │ streaming
  ▼
WAL Receiver
  │
  ▼
Standby WAL
  │
  ▼
WAL Replay
  │
  ▼
Standby database
⭐ Physical streaming replication = WAL-based replication from primary to standby.
12.2 LSNs
LSN	Meaning
sent_lsn	WAL sent by primary up to this point.
write_lsn	WAL received and written to standby WAL files.
flush_lsn	WAL flushed/synced to durable storage on standby.
replay_lsn	WAL actually replayed/applied on standby.
A gap between sent and write points toward network/receiving delay; write→flush points toward standby storage latency; flush→replay points toward replay/CPU/read-workload pressure.
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_replication;
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_wal_receiver;
SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();
12.3 Basic physical replication setup
wal_level = replica
archive_mode = on
wal_keep_size = 100MB        # example from notes; choose based on workload
wal_log_hints = on            # useful for pg_rewind

CREATE ROLE repuser WITH REPLICATION LOGIN PASSWORD '...';

# pg_hba.conf on primary:
host replication repuser <standby-address>/<mask> scram-sha-256

pg_basebackup -D /dba_instance/standby   -U repuser -p 5432 -h <primary> -P -R
•	A standby.signal file indicates standby/recovery mode for modern PostgreSQL.
•	The standby connects using primary_conninfo.
•	By default, streaming replication is asynchronous unless synchronous replication is configured.
•	Multiple standbys use WAL sender processes on the primary and WAL receiver processes on each standby.
12.4 Replication slots
A replication slot retains WAL required by a standby or logical subscriber. This prevents required WAL from being recycled before the consumer receives it.
Benefit	Risk
Standby can reconnect and continue from retained WAL without an immediate new base backup.	A long-down/inactive consumer can cause pg_wal to grow until disk space is exhausted.
SELECT slot_name, active, restart_lsn
FROM pg_replication_slots;
⭐ Replication slot = protects required WAL, but a neglected slot can fill the primary's disk.
12.5 Common replication lag causes
•	Network/receive delay.
•	Standby disk I/O bottleneck.
•	Standby CPU/replay bottleneck.
•	Heavy read workload causing replay conflicts/delay.
•	Primary generating WAL faster than standby can receive/replay.
•	Replication slot/WAL retention problems.
•	Storage capacity or infrastructure problems.
12.6 Failover and pg_rewind
After a standby is promoted, the old primary may have diverged onto a different timeline. pg_rewind can bring the old primary back in sync with the new primary by copying changed blocks from the divergence point, avoiding a full base backup when prerequisites are satisfied.
⭐ pg_rewind = rejoin old primary after failover, when the old and new timelines diverged.
The old server generally needs either data checksums enabled or wal_log_hints enabled so changed blocks can be identified.
13. Logical Replication
Concept	Meaning
Publisher	Source side where publications are defined.
Publication	Defines which tables/operations are published.
Subscriber	Destination side that subscribes to publications.
Table sync worker	Used during initial table synchronization.
Apply worker	Applies logical changes on subscriber.
Replication slot	Tracks logical decoding progress.
•	Logical replication replicates row-level changes rather than the whole physical database.
•	Table structure is not automatically reproduced as a physical copy; the target schema must be prepared appropriately.
•	Primary keys are important for identifying rows for UPDATE/DELETE in common configurations.
•	pglogical is an extension that provides additional logical-replication capabilities.
14. Backup, Restore & PITR
14.1 Logical vs physical
Backup	What it contains	Use
pg_dump	Logical objects/data for one database.	Migration, selective restore, logical backup.
pg_dumpall	Cluster-wide logical SQL, including global objects.	Logical cluster-level dump.
pg_basebackup	Physical base backup of a PostgreSQL cluster.	Standby creation, physical restore/PITR foundation.
pgBackRest	Physical backup/restore platform with full/diff/incr, WAL archive and PITR features.	Production backup/DR.
⭐ PITR needs a physical base backup + continuous WAL archiving/replay. pg_dump alone cannot provide physical WAL-based PITR.
14.2 PITR flow
PRIMARY
  │
  ├── Base backup (pg_basebackup / pgBackRest)
  │
  └── WAL archive
          │
          ▼
      RESTORE SERVER
          │
          ├── restore base backup
          ├── restore_command
          ├── recovery.signal
          └── recovery_target_time
                    │
                    ▼
               WAL replay
                    │
                    ▼
              target timestamp
                    │
                    ▼
                pg_promote()
14.3 Practical PITR steps
archive_mode = on
archive_command = 'scp %p osuser@archive-server:/archive/%f'

SELECT pg_switch_wal();

pg_basebackup -p 5432 -h <primary> -D /backup/basebackup -P -c fast

# On restore server:
restore_command = 'cp /archive/%f %p'
recovery_target_time = '2026-09-06 11:59:00'
touch recovery.signal

SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();

# After validating the recovered point:
SELECT pg_promote();
•	restore_command: %f is the WAL filename requested; %p is the destination path PostgreSQL provides.
•	log_statement='ddl' logs DDL such as CREATE/ALTER/DROP/TRUNCATE; it does not log INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.
•	pg_wal_replay_resume() resumes paused replay; it does not itself end recovery. pg_promote() promotes a recovered server to normal read-write operation.
14.4 Accidental table drop scenario
•	Restore the physical base backup to a separate recovery server.
•	Configure WAL restore and the recovery target before the destructive event.
•	Replay WAL until just before/at the required point.
•	Verify with pg_is_in_recovery() and validate the target data.
•	Export only the required object/data if needed and restore it to production.
•	Avoid using PITR directly on production when a separate recovery server can isolate the operation.
15. pgBackRest
15.1 Core concepts
Concept	Remember
Stanza	Identity/name for a PostgreSQL cluster configuration in pgBackRest.
Archive-push	Moves WAL into the pgBackRest repository.
Full	Complete backup.
Differential	Changes since the last full backup.
Incremental	Changes since the latest applicable backup.
Retention	Controls how many backups are retained.
Restore	Restores a physical backup to a target data directory.
PITR	Restore a backup and replay WAL to a target time.
[global]
repo1-path=/dba_instance/dbrepo
repo1-retention-full=2
process-max=2
start-fast=y

[main]
pg1-path=/var/lib/pgsql/17/data
pg1-port=5432

archive_command = 'pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p'

pgbackrest --stanza=main stanza-create
pgbackrest --stanza=main backup --type=full
pgbackrest --stanza=main backup --type=diff
pgbackrest --stanza=main backup --type=incr

pgbackrest info --stanza=main
pgbackrest --stanza=main restore
pgbackrest --stanza=main --set=<backup-set> restore
pgbackrest --stanza=main --type=time --target="YYYY-MM-DD HH:MI:SS" restore
⭐ pgBackRest = production-oriented physical backup + WAL archive + restore + PITR platform.
15.2 Backup verification
A backup is not trustworthy until it has been restored/tested. Periodically restore to a test/DR environment and validate PostgreSQL startup, data availability and required objects.
16. PgBouncer Connection Pooling
16.1 Why PgBouncer?
PostgreSQL uses a process per connection. Large numbers of client connections can create memory and process overhead. PgBouncer keeps many client connections while maintaining a smaller pool of PostgreSQL server connections.
Setting	Meaning
max_client_conn	Maximum client connections accepted by PgBouncer.
default_pool_size	Default number of server connections per user/database pool.
reserve_pool_size	Extra server connections allowed during bursts.
reserve_pool_timeout	How long before reserve connections can be used.
max_db_connections	Hard cap for backend connections to a database.
16.2 Pooling modes
Mode	Behavior	Important limitation
session	Backend connection stays with client until disconnect.	Highest compatibility; lower pooling efficiency.
transaction	Backend connection is returned after COMMIT/ROLLBACK.	Session-level state such as SET, LISTEN/NOTIFY, prepared statements and session advisory locks can cause problems.
statement	Connection returned after each statement.	Most restrictive; not suitable for transaction-dependent workloads.
⭐ Transaction pooling = excellent connection reuse, but do not assume the same PostgreSQL backend belongs to the client across transactions.
16.3 PgBouncer troubleshooting
•	SHOW POOLS — look for cl_waiting and server connection usage.
•	SHOW CLIENTS — inspect client-side state.
•	SHOW SERVERS — inspect actual PostgreSQL backend connections.
•	SHOW STATS — query counts and average timings.
•	High cl_waiting can mean pool exhaustion, long transactions, slow queries or undersized pools.
•	Also check PostgreSQL max_connections, max_db_connections, max_user_connections and idle-in-transaction sessions.
•	For prepared-statement issues in transaction pooling, use driver-specific workarounds, PgBouncer prepared-statement support where appropriate, or session pooling.
17. PostgreSQL High Availability
17.1 Patroni
Patroni is an HA framework for PostgreSQL. It manages leader election/failover using a distributed configuration store (DCS) and coordinates PostgreSQL roles.
Concept	Remember
Leader lock	Patroni continuously renews the leader key/lock before its TTL expires.
DCS	Distributed store used for cluster coordination; examples include etcd/Consul/ZooKeeper.
Failover	Promotion of a suitable replica when the leader is lost.
Switchover	Planned role change.
patronictl list	Quick cluster health/role view.
HAProxy	Can route client traffic toward the appropriate PostgreSQL node.
17.2 repmgr
repmgr provides PostgreSQL replication/cluster management features such as monitoring, switchover/failover and standby registration. For interviews, understand the purpose and operational workflow rather than memorizing installation commands.
17.3 HAProxy + PgBouncer
Application
    ↓
HAProxy / connection layer
    ↓
PgBouncer (optional)
    ↓
Current PostgreSQL primary
    ├── standby
    └── standby
The exact architecture depends on the HA tooling and environment.
18. Extensions & Useful PostgreSQL Tools
Extension/tool	Use
pg_buffercache	Inspect contents/status of shared buffers.
pg_stat_statements	Identify query execution statistics and expensive SQL.
pg_repack	Online table/index reorganization.
pgstattuple	Inspect table/index bloat and tuple statistics.
pg_freespacemap	Inspect free-space map information.
pg_visibility	Inspect visibility-map information.
pglogical	Additional logical replication functionality.
pg_waldump	Inspect WAL records.
pg_rewind	Rejoin an old primary after failover when prerequisites are met.
The notes also mention passwordcheck. It is a contrib module loaded through shared_preload_libraries rather than enabled with CREATE EXTENSION, and it enforces basic password-strength rules.
19. PostgreSQL Partitioning
Partitioning splits one logical table into smaller physical partitions while the application can query the parent table.
Type	Use
RANGE	Ranges such as dates or numeric ranges.
LIST	Specific values/categories.
HASH	Distribute rows based on a hash of the partition key.
CREATE TABLE orders (
    order_id bigint,
    order_date date,
    customer_id bigint
) PARTITION BY RANGE (order_date);
19.1 Partition pruning
When a query contains a condition that lets PostgreSQL identify which partitions can contain the requested rows, PostgreSQL can avoid scanning irrelevant partitions.
⭐ RANGE → dates/ranges | LIST → categories | HASH → distribution | PRUNING → skip irrelevant partitions
20. Monitoring & DBA Troubleshooting Workflow
20.1 Views to know
View	Use
pg_stat_activity	Sessions, queries, waits, transactions.
pg_stat_replication	Primary-side replication status.
pg_stat_wal_receiver	Standby-side WAL receiver status.
pg_stat_user_tables	Table activity, dead tuples, vacuum/analyze information.
pg_stat_user_indexes	Index usage statistics.
pg_locks	Locks and lock conflicts.
pg_replication_slots	Replication slot state and WAL retention position.
pg_stat_progress_vacuum	Vacuum progress.
pg_stat_wal	WAL activity.
pg_settings	Effective configuration and source/context information.
pg_database	Database-level information.
20.2 Slow query troubleshooting sequence
1. pg_stat_activity
2. Check waits / blocking / transaction age
3. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
4. Compare estimated vs actual rows
5. Check Seq Scan / Index Scan / Bitmap Scan
6. Check Nested Loop / Hash Join / Merge Join
7. Check statistics / ANALYZE
8. Check index usage and bloat
9. Check work_mem / temp spills
10. Check CPU / memory / disk I/O
11. Check recent data/config/schema changes
20.3 General database slowdown sequence
CPU / Memory
   ↓
Disk space / IOPS / latency
   ↓
Connections
   ↓
Long-running / blocked sessions
   ↓
Locks
   ↓
Slow queries / execution plans
   ↓
VACUUM / autovacuum
   ↓
Table / index bloat
   ↓
WAL generation / checkpoints
   ↓
Replication lag
   ↓
Backup status
   ↓
Logs
⭐ Do not immediately blame the query. First determine whether it is executing, waiting, blocked, or suffering from system/resource pressure.
21. AWS Basics for a PostgreSQL DBA
AWS service	DBA-level understanding
EC2	Virtual server where you manage OS + PostgreSQL yourself.
EBS	Persistent block storage used for PostgreSQL data/WAL; think volume size, IOPS, throughput and snapshots.
S3	Object storage commonly used for backup/WAL archives.
RDS PostgreSQL	Managed PostgreSQL service where AWS manages much of the infrastructure.
VPC	Network boundary with subnets.
Security Group	Network firewall controlling inbound/outbound traffic.
IAM	Controls access to AWS resources.
CloudWatch	Monitoring/metrics/alarms for AWS resources.
Interview answer: If an application server needs PostgreSQL access, allow TCP 5432 from the application's security group to the database security group rather than opening 5432 to the entire internet.
22. SQL Fundamentals & Database Objects
22.1 Keys and constraints
Constraint	One-line meaning
NOT NULL	Column must contain a value.
UNIQUE	Prevents duplicate values for the constrained key/column set; NULL behavior depends on DBMS semantics.
PRIMARY KEY	Main unique identifier; NOT NULL + unique; one PK constraint per table.
FOREIGN KEY	Enforces a relationship to a referenced key in another table.
CHECK	Value must satisfy a defined condition.
DEFAULT	Provides a value when one is not explicitly supplied.
Composite key	Key made from more than one column.
⭐ PK = identifies the row. FK = relationship. UNIQUE = no duplicate key values. CHECK = rule. DEFAULT = automatic value.
22.2 View vs stored procedure vs function vs window function
Object	Simple meaning
View	Virtual query result used to simplify/reuse/secure data access.
Stored procedure	Callable database program used to perform operations; can accept parameters and perform transactional work depending on DBMS.
Function	Returns a value/result and can be used in expressions/queries depending on DBMS.
Window function	Calculates across related rows using OVER() while keeping individual rows in the result.
⭐ Procedure → you call it to perform work. Trigger → database automatically fires it when its event occurs.
22.3 Triggers
A trigger automatically executes predefined logic when a specified event occurs.
User action
   ↓
INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE / DDL event
   ↓
Trigger fires automatically
   ↓
Audit / validation / related action
Common uses: auditing changes, automatic logging, enforcing business rules and maintaining related data. The source notes include SQL Server DML, DDL and LOGON trigger types.
23. DBA Automation: Ansible & Bash
23.1 Ansible
Concept	Interview line
Agentless	Uses remote management such as SSH rather than a persistent agent on every target.
Idempotent	Repeated execution aims to produce the same desired end state without unintended repeated changes.
Automation	Useful for configuration, patching, deployments and standardized DBA operations.
Interview answer: For a Patroni cluster patch, patch replicas one at a time, verify health after each node, then perform a controlled switchover and patch the former primary.
23.2 Bash/Linux tools
Tool	Typical DBA use
grep	Search logs/text.
awk	Extract/process columns and patterns.
sed	Text transformation.
ssh	Remote execution.
cron	Scheduled jobs.
top	CPU/process overview.
vmstat	CPU/memory/system activity.
iostat	Disk I/O.
df -h	Filesystem capacity.
free	Memory information.
24. Scenario-Based Interview Answers
24.1 Standby is lagging
Interview answer: I would first check pg_stat_replication on the primary and compare sent_lsn, write_lsn, flush_lsn and replay_lsn. Then I would check pg_stat_wal_receiver on the standby. The position of the gap tells me whether the issue is network/receive, standby storage, or WAL replay/CPU. I would also check standby CPU, disk latency, long-running queries, conflicts, WAL generation on the primary, replication slots and PostgreSQL logs.
24.2 WAL is growing rapidly
Interview answer: I would check pg_replication_slots first for inactive or stalled slots, then check replication lag and WAL archiving. I would identify whether a standby or logical subscriber is retaining WAL. I would also check pg_stat_wal and the rate of WAL generation, then address the root cause before deleting a slot. If a slot is permanently unused, it can be safely removed after confirming it is no longer required.
24.3 Query suddenly became slow
Interview answer: I would compare the current plan with a known good plan, run EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS), check estimated versus actual rows, scan type, join method, statistics, data growth and data skew. Then I would check index usage/bloat, temporary spills, CPU, memory and disk I/O. I would also verify whether the query is actually running or waiting on a lock.
24.4 Connections are exhausted
Interview answer: I would check pg_stat_activity to understand who is consuming connections and whether sessions are idle or idle in transaction. I would verify max_connections and any role/database connection limits. If the application creates many short-lived connections, I would consider PgBouncer, usually transaction pooling where the application is compatible, and then tune pool sizes based on PostgreSQL capacity.
24.5 Autovacuum is not cleaning dead tuples
Interview answer: I would identify the table and dead tuple count in pg_stat_user_tables, check pg_stat_progress_vacuum and autovacuum settings, then investigate long-running transactions, hot standby feedback, replication-related retention and whether autovacuum is disabled or unable to keep up. I would also check transaction age for wraparound risk.
24.6 Blocking session
Interview answer: I would check pg_stat_activity, wait_event and pg_blocking_pids() to identify the blocker. Then I would inspect pg_locks and the transaction age/query. Before terminating anything, I would confirm the session is not performing a critical operation. If appropriate, I would cancel the query first and terminate the backend only when necessary.
24.7 Accidental table drop
Interview answer: I would use a separate recovery server. I would restore the physical base backup, configure restore_command and recovery.signal, set the recovery target to just before the destructive event, replay WAL, verify the table, export the required object/data, and restore that data to production. I would avoid taking production offline unless there is a specific reason.
24.8 Old primary after failover
Interview answer: After the standby is promoted, the old primary is on the old timeline and may have divergent changes. If prerequisites such as data checksums or wal_log_hints are available, I would use pg_rewind to synchronize the old primary with the new primary. If pg_rewind cannot be used, I would rebuild the old primary from a new base backup.
25. High-Probability Interview Questions — One-Line Answers
Question	Answer to remember
What is PostgreSQL MVCC?	Multiple row versions allow readers and writers to work concurrently using transaction snapshots.
What is VACUUM?	Cleans dead tuples and makes space reusable; it does not normally shrink the table file.
VACUUM FULL?	Rewrites the table, returns free space to OS, and requires ACCESS EXCLUSIVE.
Why ANALYZE?	Updates statistics used by the planner.
Why autovacuum?	Automatically performs vacuum/analyze and helps prevent bloat and transaction ID wraparound.
What is work_mem?	Memory available per query operation such as sort/hash; multiple operations/sessions can consume it.
What is effective_cache_size?	Planner estimate of cache available; it does not allocate memory.
What is a replication slot?	Tracks WAL consumption and prevents required WAL from being recycled.
What is replication lag?	Standby/subscriber is behind the primary's WAL generation/replay position.
What is pg_rewind?	Re-synchronizes an old primary with the new primary after failover when prerequisites are met.
What is PITR?	Restore a physical base backup and replay WAL to a selected point in time.
pg_dump vs pg_basebackup?	pg_dump is logical; pg_basebackup is physical.
What is pgBackRest?	Physical backup/WAL archive/restore/PITR platform.
What is PgBouncer?	Lightweight PostgreSQL connection pooler.
Transaction pooling risk?	The same client may use different PostgreSQL backend sessions across transactions.
What is a B-tree?	General-purpose ordered index for equality, ranges and sorting.
What is GIN?	Inverted index useful for arrays, JSONB and multi-value/full-text searches.
What is BRIN?	Very small summary index for huge naturally ordered tables.
What is Bitmap Heap Scan?	Fetches matching heap pages based on a bitmap, often after a Bitmap Index Scan.
Nested Loop?	For each outer row, find matching inner rows.
Hash Join?	Build hash table then probe it for matching keys.
Merge Join?	Merge two sorted inputs.
What is a deadlock?	Transactions wait for each other's locks in a cycle.
What is XID wraparound?	Risk caused by reuse of 32-bit transaction IDs; freezing prevents old rows being confused with new transactions.
What is a checkpoint?	Flushes required dirty data pages and establishes a recovery point in WAL processing.
What is WAL?	Write-ahead log used for durability, crash recovery, replication and PITR.
What is Patroni?	HA framework that coordinates PostgreSQL leader election/failover using a DCS.
What is HAProxy?	Traffic-routing/load-balancing layer commonly used with PostgreSQL HA.
What is a partition?	Smaller physical piece of one logical table.
What is partition pruning?	Skipping partitions that cannot contain the requested rows.
 
26. LAST-MINUTE POSTGRESQL DBA CHEAT SHEET
⭐ Architecture: Connection → pg_hba.conf → Backend → Parse → Analyze → Rewrite → Plan → Execute
⭐ Security: pg_hba.conf = connection. GRANT/roles = authorization.
⭐ effective_cache_size = planner estimate, NOT allocated RAM.
⭐ work_mem = per operation; sort/hash can use it multiple times.
⭐ shared_buffers = PostgreSQL data cache.
⭐ EXPLAIN = estimate. EXPLAIN ANALYZE = executes and shows actuals.
⭐ shared hit = shared_buffers. shared read = had to read page. dirtied = changed. written = flushed during query.
⭐ Estimated rows ≠ actual rows → check statistics / ANALYZE / data skew.
⭐ Seq Scan vs Index Scan vs Bitmap Scan: planner chooses based on estimated cost/selectivity.
⭐ Nested Loop = repeated inner lookup. Hash = build + probe. Merge = sorted inputs.
⭐ B-tree = general. GIN = multi-value. GiST = overlap/distance/range. BRIN = huge ordered data.
⭐ VACUUM = reuse space. VACUUM FULL = rewrite + return OS space + exclusive lock. ANALYZE = planner stats.
⭐ VM = visibility. FSM = free space.
⭐ MVCC = multiple row versions; xmin/xmax are key hidden columns.
⭐ XID wraparound → freezing is required.
⭐ WAL = durability + crash recovery + replication + PITR.
⭐ Replication LSN order: sent → write → flush → replay.
⭐ Replication slot protects required WAL; abandoned slot can fill pg_wal.
⭐ pg_rewind = old primary → new primary timeline after failover.
⭐ PITR = base backup + WAL archive + target time.
⭐ pgBackRest = physical backup/restore/WAL/PITR platform.
⭐ PgBouncer = connection pooling; transaction pooling can break session state.
⭐ Patroni = HA/failover; DCS = cluster coordination; HAProxy = traffic routing.
⭐ Partitioning: RANGE / LIST / HASH; pruning skips irrelevant partitions.
⭐ Core monitoring: pg_stat_activity, pg_stat_replication, pg_stat_wal_receiver, pg_stat_user_tables, pg_stat_user_indexes, pg_locks, pg_replication_slots.
⭐ Slow query workflow: activity → waits/locks → EXPLAIN ANALYZE BUFFERS → estimates vs actuals → scans/joins → stats/indexes → resources.
⭐ WAL growth workflow: replication slots → archive → standby lag → WAL generation → disk space.
⭐ Connection issue workflow: pg_stat_activity → idle/idle-in-transaction → limits → PgBouncer → pool sizing.
⭐ Backup rule: never assume a backup works until you have restored/tested it.
The 10 concepts you should explain without hesitation
1. PostgreSQL query processing flow
2. MVCC and dead tuples
3. VACUUM vs VACUUM FULL vs ANALYZE
4. EXPLAIN ANALYZE + BUFFERS
5. Index Scan vs Bitmap Scan vs Seq Scan
6. Nested Loop vs Hash Join vs Merge Join
7. WAL + checkpoints
8. Physical streaming replication + LSNs + slots
9. PITR / pgBackRest
10. Patroni + PgBouncer + pg_rewind
Final interview mindset
•	Start with what you would check, not only the definition.
•	Use the relevant PostgreSQL view/command to prove the problem.
•	Separate symptom from root cause.
•	Mention impact: CPU, memory, disk, locks, WAL, replication or downtime.
•	For risky operations, mention validation, maintenance window, backup and rollback.
•	When an interviewer gives an ambiguous scenario, clarify the exact topology and failure condition before choosing a solution.
